<BaseLayout title="Research">
  <div class="eyebrow">Research / Evaluation protocol</div>
  <h1>What would count as a real improvement?</h1>
  <p class="lead">A faster training run is only useful evidence if the work and model quality remain comparable—and the gains survive unfamiliar settings.</p>
  <div class="grid">
    <section class="card"><h2>1. Hold work constant</h2><p>Every configuration must process the same tokens per optimizer step. Reducing the work does not count as a speedup.</p></section>
    <section class="card"><h2>2. Check model quality</h2><p>Compare validation loss at a fixed checkpoint. A large loss regression invalidates the apparent throughput gain.</p></section>
    <section class="card"><h2>3. Use baselines</h2><p>Compare the proposed agent against an untouched setup, manual tuning and non-LLM search under the same proposal budget.</p></section>
    <section class="card"><h2>4. Protect held-out tasks</h2><p>Finalize the agent's prompts and search policy on development tasks before evaluating unseen model and GPU combinations.</p></section>
  </div>
  <section class="card">
    <h2>Current status and limitations</h2>
    <p>The first Kaggle T4 GPU measurement pilot completed successfully and its real results appear in <a href="/experiments">Experiments</a>. It used a small model, synthetic data, two seeds and only three timed optimizer steps per configuration. It is evidence that the execution and data-collection workflow works, not evidence that an agent discovers generalizable speedups.</p>
    <p>The full GPT-2 benchmark entrypoint and the LLM agent remain unfinished. Timing checks are currently in-process and do not prove isolation from agent-controlled code.</p>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/mbzuaiciao/throughput-agent">Inspect the source and open issues</a></p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
